Subject: [PATCH 1/2] decouple waypoint spacing from sim step, and render only the cameras a model uses

Two knobs NeuroNCAP does not expose upstream, both needed to compare models
fairly on one GPU.

`--engine.waypoint-dt` separates the rate the simulation is stepped at from the
grid the model plans on. They are the same thing upstream. They must differ here
because openpilot needs 20 Hz input and reads a 0.5 s step as a parked car, so
every model is driven at a 0.1 s sim step to keep control authority equal --
while the waypoint grid stays at 0.5 s, which the collision lookahead and the
reference-speed calculation both hard-code.

`--engine.cameras` restricts what is rendered each step. Rendering dominates
wall-clock and is linear in camera count, so rendering only what a model
consumes is the largest speed-up available. Scoring is unaffected. The logger
change follows from it: a debug panel with no image behind it is left blank
rather than failing the step.

Upstreamable in principle; both are additive and default to current behaviour.

diff --git a/neuro_ncap/components/logger.py b/neuro_ncap/components/logger.py
index a397c76..8804b15 100644
--- a/neuro_ncap/components/logger.py
+++ b/neuro_ncap/components/logger.py
@@ -145,6 +145,13 @@ class NCAPLogger:
         obj_ax = fig.add_subplot(gs[:3, :3])
 
         for cam, ax in zip(["CAM_FRONT", "CAM_FRONT_LEFT", "CAM_FRONT_RIGHT"], [fc_ax, fl_ax, fr_ax]):
+            # A run may render only the cameras its model consumes, so a panel
+            # here can have no image behind it. Leave that panel blank rather
+            # than failing the step -- this is a debug view, and losing it must
+            # not cost the run.
+            if cam not in images:
+                ax.axis("off")
+                continue
             img = Image.fromarray((images[cam].cpu().numpy()))
             canvas = ImageDraw.Draw(img)
             canvas.text((img.width // 2 - len(cam) * 20, 800), cam, fill=(0, 255, 0), font=FONT_BIG)
diff --git a/neuro_ncap/engine.py b/neuro_ncap/engine.py
index 70de426..4cd95fe 100644
--- a/neuro_ncap/engine.py
+++ b/neuro_ncap/engine.py
@@ -48,6 +48,25 @@ class EngineConfig(InstantiateConfig):
     """Configuration for the evaluator."""
     scenario: ScenarioConfig = field(default_factory=ScenarioConfig)
     """Configuration for the scenario."""
+    waypoint_dt: float | None = None
+    """Spacing of the waypoints the model returns, in seconds.
+
+    Defaults to the scenario time step, which is what the model is asked to
+    plan at when the two rates are the same. They only differ when the
+    simulation is stepped faster than 2 Hz to suit a model that needs a higher
+    frame rate: the ego then moves in smaller increments while the model keeps
+    planning on the 0.5 s grid the rest of the codebase assumes (the collision
+    lookahead and the reference-speed calculation both hard-code 0.5 s). Note
+    that six waypoints at a 0.1 s spacing only reach 0.6 s ahead, which is
+    shorter than the LQR tracker's 1.0 s lookahead.
+    """
+    cameras: tuple[str, ...] | None = None
+    """Which cameras to render each step. Defaults to all six.
+
+    Rendering dominates the wall-clock cost of a run, and it is linear in the
+    number of cameras, so restricting this to the cameras a model actually
+    consumes is the single largest speed-up available. Scoring is unaffected.
+    """
 
 
 class Engine:
@@ -62,7 +81,7 @@ class Engine:
         self.evaluator: Evaluator = None  # will be reinitizalized
         self.scenario: Scenario = config.scenario.setup()
 
-        self.cam_names = [
+        self.cam_names = list(config.cameras) if config.cameras else [
             "CAM_FRONT",
             "CAM_FRONT_LEFT",
             "CAM_FRONT_RIGHT",
@@ -247,7 +266,8 @@ class Engine:
             state.is_in_auto_mode = True
 
         previous_pose = state.ego2world
-        trajectory_times = [state.timestamp + int(i * self.timestep * 1e6) for i in range(1, 7)]
+        waypoint_dt = self.config.waypoint_dt or self.timestep
+        trajectory_times = [state.timestamp + int(i * waypoint_dt * 1e6) for i in range(1, 7)]
         for _ in range(self._vehicle_model_sim_steps):
             state.ego2world = self.vehicle_model.step(
                 state.ego2world, self._vehicle_model_discretization_time, trajectory, trajectory_times
